WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:08.980
The design of this shoe starts in Oregon in 1981 and with a man called Tinker Hatfield.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:14.300
He was an architect for Nike, hired to draw up offices and showrooms on the company’s campus.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.500
After making a statement on the outside
of buildings,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.440
he was asked to join the designers on the inside.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.919
In a race to develop sneakers, a former NASA engineer had recently introduced the idea of sealing

00:00:26.919 --> 00:00:30.349
air bubbles in the soles for extra comfort
and performance.

00:00:30.349 --> 00:00:35.680
But competition was fierce and Nike still
needed an edge.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.070
Hatfield headed to Paris in search of inspiration.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:44.000
While he was there he visited the Pompidou Centre, a radical building famous for

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.460
its inside out design, which exposed the building’s components like escalators

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.420
and heating pipes for everyone to see.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.370
Hatfield decided to apply the same principle to his own design, revealing the inner workings

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.760
of his shoe by making the air cushioning system visible.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.640
The marketing team thought the air bubble would seem like a weakness,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.920
but the Nike Air Max 1 was an instant hit when it launched in 1987.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.940
Originally pairs sold for $75, now they
go for double that.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:22.560
Today Tinker Hatfield is one of the most successful shoes designers in the world.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.800
He even created those legendary self-lacing sneakers of Back to the Future fame.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.660
Stylish and innovative, this is the design
that stayed one step ahead,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.000
simply by being turned inside out.

